<p>
  The internet is full of tennis elbow 'fixes' — braces, ultrasound, dry needling, cortisone shots, and endless stretching videos. Some of these have a role. Most are doing the heavy lifting for you while the real work goes undone.
</p>

<p>
  This post gives you the approach that the evidence actually supports: progressive loading of the forearm extensor tendons, combined with smart load management. It's not glamorous. It requires consistency. And it works.
</p>


<h2>Step 1: Reduce the Provocative Load</h2>

<p>
  You can't out-exercise a tendon that's constantly being re-irritated. The first step is identifying the activities that provoke the most pain and temporarily modifying them. This doesn't mean stopping work or life — it means making smart adjustments:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Keyboard and mouse: raise your keyboard so your wrist is neutral, not extended. Consider a vertical mouse.</li>
  <li>Gripping tools: use padded grips to reduce the force required. Take regular breaks during repetitive grip tasks.</li>
  <li>Lifting: use a two-handed neutral-grip (palm facing up on one hand) for carrying objects.</li>
  <li>Racquet or club sports: consider a temporary break during the acute loading phase, or modify technique.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  The goal is a tendon that's irritable enough to adapt to your loading programme, but not so constantly provoked that it can't recover between sessions.
</p>


<h2>Step 2: Isometric Wrist Extension Exercises</h2>

<p>
  Start with isometrics: contracting the wrist extensor muscles without moving the wrist. Isometrics are the safest entry point for irritated tendons and have demonstrated immediate pain-reducing effects.
</p>

<p>
  How to perform:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Sit with your forearm resting on a table, palm facing down</li>
  <li>Place your other hand on the back of your affected hand</li>
  <li>Push gently downward with the resting hand while resisting with the wrist extensors — no movement</li>
  <li>Hold for 30-45 seconds with moderate-to-firm contraction</li>
  <li>Rest 2 minutes. Repeat 4-5 times.</li>
  <li>Perform once or twice daily</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Aim for 3-4/10 discomfort during the hold — not pain-free, not screaming.
</p>


<h2>Step 3: Heavy Slow Resistance Loading</h2>

<p>
  After 1-2 weeks of isometrics (or sooner if the tendon is less irritable), progress to the main driver of tendon recovery: heavy slow resistance (HSR) loading of the wrist extensors.
</p>

<p>
  The eccentric-concentric wrist extension protocol:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Sit with forearm supported on a table, hand and wrist off the edge, palm facing down</li>
  <li>Hold a light dumbbell (start with 0.5-1 kg) in your hand</li>
  <li>Slowly lower the wrist (3-4 seconds down), then slowly raise it back (3-4 seconds up)</li>
  <li>3 sets of 15 reps. Rest 2-3 minutes between sets.</li>
  <li>Progress the weight when 3x15 is comfortable — add 0.5-1 kg increments</li>
  <li>Perform every other day</li>
</ul>

<p>
  The tempo is critical. Slow, controlled loading drives tendon adaptation. Fast, bouncy reps don't.
</p>


<h2>Step 4: Grip and Functional Strengthening</h2>

<p>
  As the tendon responds, incorporate functional strengthening:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Towel wringing: hold a damp towel with both hands and wring it slowly</li>
  <li>Stress ball or putty: progressive gripping exercises 2-3 times daily</li>
  <li>Pronation-supination with weight: hold a hammer or weighted object and slowly rotate forearm from palm-down to palm-up</li>
  <li>Wrist radial/ulnar deviation exercises with light resistance</li>
</ul>


<h2>Step 5: Address the Contributing Factors</h2>

<p>
  Loading the tendon without fixing what caused the problem produces slower, more fragile results. Address:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Workstation ergonomics — the single most impactful change for desk-worker tennis elbow</li>
  <li>Technique in sport or trade — poor mechanics often shift load disproportionately onto the lateral elbow</li>
  <li>Overall forearm strength — a stronger forearm distributes load better across the whole arm</li>
</ul>


<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>


<h3>Should I use a tennis elbow strap?</h3>

<p>
  A counterforce strap can reduce symptoms during activity in the early phases and help you stay active during recovery. It works by shifting load away from the tendon origin. Use it as a short-term adjunct, not a permanent solution.
</p>


<h3>Is massage helpful for tennis elbow?</h3>

<p>
  Deep friction massage (cross-fibre massage) has some evidence for short-term symptom relief. Myofascial release and general soft tissue work to the forearm can reduce muscle tension and improve function. Manual therapy is most useful as a supporting measure alongside loading, not as a standalone treatment.
</p>


<h3>What if my tennis elbow isn't improving after 6 weeks?</h3>

<p>
  Reassess your programme. Are you genuinely progressing the load? Are you addressing ergonomics? If you've followed a proper programme consistently and aren't improving, it's worth clinical assessment to rule out other diagnoses and consider whether additional interventions (PRP, shockwave) are warranted.
</p>


